  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday night through Friday: Drier air currently in place has \nbrought clearing to much of eastern Washington and north Idaho this \nafternoon. Through the end of the workweek, the Pacific Northwest \nwill remain under mostly zonal flow aloft. This will allow for \ntemperatures to gradually trend warmer, while also allowing embedded \nweak systems and moisture within the flow to move through. Moisture \nincreases again tonight bringing more middle and high level clouds. \nBefore the moisture lifts northward, clear skies and light winds \nearly Wednesday night will allow low temperatures across the \nnorthern valleys to plummet into the 30s. Uncertainty exists in how \nquickly the temperatures can decrease before cloud cover increases, \nwhich will limit the radiative cooling. These weak embedded waves \nbring a 15-25% chance for very light rain showers across the far \nnorth Cascades Thursday afternoon and Friday, with the rest of the \nInland Northwest only seeing passing clouds.\n\nFriday through Tuesday: Ensembles are in good agreement for the \nridge to become established over the Pacific Northwest this weekend, \nwith temperatures trending to 10-15 degrees above normal by Sunday \nand Monday (highs in the mid 70s to low 80s). Models hint at a weak \nweather system Monday into Tuesday temporarily flattening the ridge, \nwith subtle cooling on Tuesday. Beyond Tuesday, models continue to \nbe in agreement for the upper-level ridge to amplify for the rest \nof the work week keeping the Inland Northwest warm and dry. /vmt",
